WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.260
Template engines and expression languages are interpreters.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:15.400
Java applications may use technologies such as JSP expression language, Thymeleaf, FreeMarker, Velocity, Spring Expression Language, or framework-specific expression features.

3
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.300
These tools are useful because they evaluate templates, conditions, and expressions.

4
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:31.360
They become risky when user-controlled input can influence template logic, expression text, script evaluation, or dynamic behavior that was intended to be controlled by developers.

5
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:39.480
Server-side template injection is best understood at a high level as user content crossing into template instruction space.

6
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:47.100
A comment, display name, search term, or message body should be rendered as content, not parsed as a template.

7
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:56.400
Similarly, expression language features should receive controlled developer expressions, not arbitrary strings from requests, imports, or stored data.

8
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:03.540
Sandboxes and restricted modes can help, but they should not be treated as permission to accept untrusted expressions.

9
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:11.500
Dynamic scripting, plugin systems, rule engines, and configuration-driven behavior need clear trust boundaries.

10
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:18.880
If the product allows administrators to define rules or templates, separate that trusted administrative function from normal user input.

11
00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:27.060
Review dependencies that interpret strings, evaluate expressions, deserialize behavior, or load classes dynamically.

12
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:32.920
A harmless-looking helper can become a sensitive sink when it converts text into executable logic.

13
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.600
The defensive pattern is separation.

14
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.660
Store user content as content.

15
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.640
Store developer-controlled templates separately.

16
00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:50.060
Use template APIs that escape by default for the relevant context, and only disable escaping after a deliberate review.

17
00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:58.160
Avoid giving users control over template names, expression bodies, class names, method names, or evaluation context.

18
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:05.940
When dynamic behavior is required, constrain it to a small vocabulary of approved operations rather than open-ended evaluation.
